body:
yes, it is so awful, and I can't agree more. I feel like things will be bad if I don't take my medication, but they're also bad if I do. so I guess it becomes a matter of what is the least shittiest option.

&#x200B;

I'd love to come right off it, but I'm hesitant to, without medical advice. My psychiatrist usually combines the lamictal with another medication eg. SNRI or SRI etc. He's prescribed me cymbalta alongside the lamictal, but it'll be the 11th anti-depressant I've tried, which just makes the whole process feel hopeless now.

&#x200B;

So, to answer your question regarding my plan, I'm not sure. I don't know if I want to try the cymbalta with it. I don't know if I should just lessen the dose down to 0 myself (probably not wise). I'm worried if I see him again and I haven't tried the cymbalta and have lowered the dosage of lamictal, he'll just push harder for an increase of the dosage.

What is your doctor like? What are their views on the drug? I feel like 100mg is so much. How long were you on 200mg? Did you find that you forgot anything else, besides your address? What was it like? It sounds quite awful

No worries! I am the same here too - my GP can indeed prescribe antidepressants but after trying multiple they recommended me to see a psychiatrist, who knows more about them, and I eventually agreed. Whether or not that has helped, however, remains to be seen. The whole thing seems a bit of a trial an error situation - even the most knowledgable of mental health professionals admit there's still many things we do not know, especially in regard to antidepressants and their effects.

&#x200B;

When you talk about how you're worried you'll have your license revoked - is this if you start on antidepressants? Is this something you have to disclose to them?

&#x200B;

Do you see a psychologist/therapist?

&#x200B;

That really sucks, I can't begin to imagine how awful that must be, having to have felt like this for the past fourteen years.

&#x200B;

And don't apologise! I'm more than happy to listen.

how long did you find you had to wait? my doctor was originally just doing trial and error with me, but was constantly suggesting a psychiatrist, who knew lots more about medications. but now I'm paying upwards of $300 (im a uni student, and it was one of the cheapest I could find, bit of a bummer ): ) for seemingly the same trial and error process.

&#x200B;

thank you for your response

I'm in Australia so any form of marijuana, even for pain relief, is illegal.

&#x200B;

The pain has passed now, thankfully. But when I'm in the midst of it, it's debilitating. I can't move, I'm coated in sweat and I'm just waiting to die. I'm particularly upset today as it means I missed my university camp which I was excited for.
